From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] opengl32: send immediate mode calls across in batches

Purpose: a 32-bit OpenGL program that draws in immediate mode spends its
time in the call, not in the driver.
Wine before: every GL call is a unix call. In a WoW64 process each one
switches from 32-bit to 64-bit code and back, and on a CPU without
FSGSBASE (the EeePC's Atom N550) __wine_unix_call_dispatcher sets FS with
an arch_prctl system call each time. Immediate mode (glBegin, then
glTexCoord/glColor/glVertex for every vertex, then glEnd) makes one call
per attribute.
Change: UNIX_CALL goes through opengl_unix_call(). glBegin, glEnd and the
glVertex2f/3f, glTexCoord2f, glColor3f/4f/3ub/4ub, glNormal3f (and their
vector forms) and glMultiTexCoord2fARB calls are not sent: their params
are copied into a 16 KB buffer per thread (the vector forms as the scalar
forms, so no pointer into the caller's memory is kept). Any other call,
and a full buffer, first sends them all with one unix call, gl_batch,
which runs each through the same native or WoW64 thunk as before, in
order. So the GL sees the same calls in the same order, and glGetError,
glGet*, wglMakeCurrent and SwapBuffers see their effects. The buffer is
found through the TEB's glReserved1[6] (only while calls are waiting)
and [7]; neither is used by Wine. WINE_OPENGL_BATCH=0 turns it off.
Evidence: Half-Life Alpha on the EeePC (perf record, 15 s of play): about
59% of the render thread in the unix call path, 37% in Mesa and the
driver; 181,000 arch_prctl calls a second, against a few hundred from
the rest of the session (tools/eeepc/README.md). With the patch:
10,000 arch_prctl calls a second instead of 361,000, and Mesa and the
driver's share of the render thread up from about 37% to 60%.
tools/eeepc/glbench.c draws the same image with and without it.
Scope: all programs; only how the listed calls reach the Unix side
changes.
---
